You can change FastAPI to deserialize JSON by default using `TypeAdapter.dump_json(model)` by setting the `json` parameter of the `ResponseModel` to `json` instead of `jsonable_encoder`. 

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, ResponseModel
from pydantic import BaseModel, TypeAdapter

class Item(BaseModel):
    name: str
    description: str | None = None

class ItemAdapter(TypeAdapter[Item]):
    def __init__(self, model: Item):
        self.model = model

    def dump_json(self, obj: Item, **kwargs) -> str:
        return obj.json()

app = FastAPI()

@app.get("/items/")
async def read_items():
    item = Item(name="Foo", description="A very nice Item")
    return ResponseModel(ItemAdapter(item).dump_json())
```

You can declare a query parameter with `Annotated` and `Query` to add validation, but it will not work as expected when used with repeated query parameters. To fix this, you can use `Query(..., min_length=1)` directly in the route function or in model class. 

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Query
from pydantic import BaseModel
from typing import Annotated, MinLen

app = FastAPI()

class QueryModel(BaseModel):
    descriptions: Annotated[list[str], MinLen(1)] | None = None

@app.get("/test")
def test(query: QueryModel = Query(min_length=1)):
    return {"descriptions": query.descriptions}
```

